.TH DEDUPCTL 1 2026-11-08 "dedupctl" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dedupctl \- encrypted, compressed BorgBackup archives of a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY dedupctl
[\fB\-h\fR]
\fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARG\fR\~.\|.\|.]
.YS
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dedupctl
keeps one Borg repository per backed-up directory.
.B init
marks a directory as a backup source by writing a
.I .borgbackup
file into it, see
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5),
and creates the repository under
.BR $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT ,
named after the directory's absolute path with every
.B /
turned into
.B !
(and every
.B !
doubled), encrypted with
.BR repokey .
.PP
Every other command looks for the nearest
.I .borgbackup
in the current directory or its parents, changes into that directory,
and reads the file.
A command that needs an archive and is given none lists the archives and
asks for one by number.
.PP
Arguments are read only after the command; the dispatcher itself knows
only
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
Every command refuses an option it does not know with status 2 before it
does anything, and every command takes
.BR \-h " and " \-\-help .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show this help message and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS "init"
.TP
\fBdedupctl init\fR [<path>]
.PP
Initialize a Borg backup repository for <path> (or the current directory if not provided). This creates a .borgbackup configuration file and initializes the repository with encryption (repokey). It takes no options.
.SS "backup"
.TP
\fBdedupctl backup\fR [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR]
.PP
Create a new backup archive for the repository defined in the current directory (or its nearest parent). Archives are created using compression (lz4) and stored in the repository. A parent directory's repository, if there is one, is then backed up with the same options. Options:
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Simulate the backup; nothing is written, here or in a parent repository.
.PP
The archive is named after the time it starts,
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD_HH:MM:SS ,
and is written with
.B borg create \-\-compression lz4
from
.BR SOURCE_DIR ,
leaving out the
.B EXCLUDES
of
.IR .borgbackup .
Its output also goes to
.IR $BORG_REPO/logs/<time>.log .
.B \-\-dry\-run
is passed to
.B borg create
and writes no log, so the repository is left as it was.
.PP
When a directory above the repository root has a
.I .borgbackup
of its own, the backup then runs there too, with the same options: a
.B \-\-dry\-run
stays a dry run in the parent.
.SS "prune"
.TP
\fBdedupctl prune\fR [options]
.PP
Purge backup archives. Options (mutually exclusive):
.TP
\fB\-\-last\fR X
Remove the newest X archives.
.TP
\fB\-\-first\fR X
Remove the oldest X archives.
.TP
\fB\-\-older\fR X
Remove archives older than X days.
.TP
\fB\-\-newer\fR X
Remove archives newer than X days.
.TP
\fB\-\-all\fR
Remove all archives.
.PP
If no option is given, interactive mode is used.
.PP
Each archive is removed with
.BR "borg delete" .
In interactive mode the archives are listed and the numbers to remove
are read, or
.BR all ;
both modes ask for confirmation first.
.SS "mount"
.TP
\fBdedupctl mount\fR \%[<mount_point>] [<archive>]
.PP
Mount a backup archive from the repository.
.PP
The mount point is created when missing.
Unmount with
.B borg umount
.I mount_point
or
.BR umount (8).
.SS "extract"
.TP
\fBdedupctl extract\fR [<destination>] [<archive>]
.PP
Extract a backup archive from the repository to a destination directory.
.SS "check"
.TP
\fBdedupctl check\fR
.PP
Perform an integrity check of the repository using 'borg check'.
.SS "log"
.TP
\fBdedupctl log\fR [<log\-index\-or\-substring>]
.PP
Display a backup log file.
.PP
The log is shown with
.BR less (1).
.SS "list"
.TP
\fBdedupctl list\fR
.PP
List all available backup archives in the repository.
.SS "diff"
.TP
\fBdedupctl diff\fR
.PP
Compare two archives. For the first prompt, if you press Enter without entering a number, the live state (current directory) is used.
.PP
Two archives are compared with
.BR "borg diff" .
The live state is compared by extracting the archive into a temporary
directory, removed afterwards, and running
.B diff \-r
against it.
.SS "size"
.TP
\fBdedupctl size\fR
.PP
Display the size and info of an archive (using 'borg info').
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, also
.BR \-h " / " \-\-help
after any command or none, and
.B init
on a directory that already has a
.IR .borgbackup .
.TP
.B 1
No command or an unknown command (the usage text goes to standard
error), no
.I .borgbackup
was found, a selection was invalid, or another error.
A failing
.B borg
command ends the run with its own status.
.TP
.B 2
An option the command does not know,
.B init
given an option, more than one path or a path that is not a directory,
or
.B prune
given more than one of its options, or one without its number.
.TP
.B 3
.I .borgbackup
is missing or does not set a variable the command needs.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BORG_BACKUP_ROOT
Where
.B init
creates repositories; default
.IR ~/.borg_backups .
.TP
.BR BORG_PASSPHRASE ", " BORG_PASSCOMMAND ", ..."
Read by
.B borg
itself, which every command runs; see
.BR borg (1).
.SH FILES
.TP
.I .borgbackup
The backup source's configuration, in the directory it backs up; see
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5).
.TP
.I $BORG_BACKUP_ROOT/<name>/
The repository
.B init
creates.
.TP
.I $BORG_REPO/logs/*.log
One log per backup, read by
.BR log .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dedupctl.borgbackup (5),
.BR borg (1),
.BR hlinkctl (1)
